Project Lantern v0 does not ingest or retain raw incident reports, chat messages, page timelines, comments, or unredacted operational notes as Lantern records. It may consume authorized source data to produce a service-level incident-load summary, but the raw payload is not copied into Lantern.

Users see only permission-bounded, provenance-backed summaries, such as:

- incident-load counts and severity distribution;
- the relevant service and time window;
- confidence or data-quality indicators;
- an “Open permissioned source” link when the viewer is authorized.

Lantern does not display raw incident bodies, unredacted notes, private chat, or unpermissioned status chatter. If no usable authorized source is available, it shows that the summary or source is unavailable; that must not be interpreted as proof that no incident occurred.

Retention and access for the raw material remain governed by the originating incident, chat, or operational system. Those systems retain the full context, edits, permissions, and audit history. Lantern retains its derived event/summary data and provenance metadata, subject to its internal v0 storage policies, but it is not a second incident-record system and has no separate retention or access model for raw content. Raw details remain accessible only through the source system’s existing permissions.

